en they  also have access to proper mental health  services those that recognition of  skills actually something that they sort  directly spoke about Imagineers  interesting - he say that and another  leg of party policy that was keen to  touch about is Trident now currently of  course is Labour's policy to renew  nuclear deterrent however the shoddy  peace minister had Fabien Hamilton  carrying out a review to see if  alternative jobs can be found for those  people working in the defense industry  he says he hopes that he sincerely hopes  that the review he's undertaking will  convince people to drop their support of  Trident so what's happening there okay  it's trying definitely going to be in  labour party policy going forward well  we are absolutely committed to the  Trident program the decision was taken  2,000 was taken back in 2007 and it's  settled Labour Party policy  now Fabian Hamilton can express his own  views but for the you know the the  mainstream Labour Party position on this  is that we are totally committed to the  Trident nuclear deterrent and you  mustn't muddle up the Trident nuclear  deterrent and the whole issue of  industrial diversification yeah but at  the same time though this is one of your  junior Shadow Cabinet ministers may he  does speak for the party and he's very  clear that he says that you know he's  always said that party policy should  renew tried it but I say we should scrap  it that's also the view the leaders a  party and he sincerely hopes review he's  undertaking will convince unions to drop  their support as well well as I say  they're not very helpful if you if  you're wildly in favor of keeping quiet  there are two very separate issues first  and foremost we are committed to the  deterrent that turns is extremely  important it's a very important part of  our defence policy and so we went there  we made the decision to renew it back in  2007 and obviously that's now eleven  years ago and the process is well down  the line now in terms of defence  diversification we have taught in a very  different way about industrial  diversification because we recognize  that there is immense inequality across  the regions of the UK and we recognize  that part of ensuring that no region is  too dependent for example on the coal  industry or the steel industry is to  make sure that there is better  redistribution of opportunity so that  people in every area of the UK have good  opportunities for further career  opportunities and now I'm keen to talk  to you as well about Brad said and  Jeremy Corbyn gave an interview this  week and he was asked if he would stop  brexit if he could and we could have a  look at the answer that he gave on  brexit he said we can't stop it the  referendum took place article 50 has  been triggered is your leader right to  say though you can't stop brexit well I  think the the decision was taken back in  2016 and very clearly the people of the  UK voted to leave the European Union you  can't stop a country if you wanted to  well what we want to see is a proper  deal because I know that many of those  people who voted leave certainly did not  want to see jobs and the economy  decimated they wanted to see it breaks  it that were  for jobs in the economy not the sort of  factionism we're seeing in the Tory  Party now at the same time though I  can't help but thinking that if this is  right that Jeremy Corbyn believes you  can't stop brexit then the motion you  pass at your conference was basically a  bit of a con because that said if we  cannot get a general election labour  must support all options remaining on  the table including campaigning for a  public vote but your leader is saying  that brexit can't be stopped  well those things don't add up to that  well I think you have to take things  step by step we've said very very  clearly we do not want a situation of no  deal we've also said very clear that we  cannot be voting for a bad deal or a  blind breaks it so what we are saying is  we need to resume now to come back with  a proper deal which protects jobs and  the economy which gives us that closest  possible relationship to the single  market and that's the sort of deal we  want to see on the table so any hand  brexit Lee stops well I think the the  answer to that is that we are we are  committed to getting a breaks if that  works we want to break it that works in  terms of jobs and the economy we are  prepared as Kier summers set out today  to work with MPs across the board to get  something which is workable and that's  where we are at this present time we  want to make sure is a second referendum  one of the options on the table well in  our in our view what we actually need to  do is get a proper deal that works now  if this government is incapable of  negotiating that deal then the best  option as far as we're concerned would  be to throw the government out and have  a team in there which can get a good  deal in other words we'd like to have a  general election if that's not an option  then we've said quite clearly we are  prepared to work across the board to try  and get a deal which would be workable  and I think the whole issue about a  people's vote arises because of the  prospect of a bad deal and I think if  for a good deal on the table that did do  as I've said protect jobs in the economy  then I think that issue would not be  half as vibrant as it is okay nee  Griffith thank you very much

